5.7 Admin Authentication

Use Supabase Auth.

Requirements:

- Secure admin sign-in.
- Sign-out.
- Session validation.
- Protected administrative routes.
- Server-side authorization for every sensitive operation.
- Secure password-reset workflow when configured.
- Clear authentication errors.

The initial system is for a single administrator.

Do not implement public registration.

Do not treat hiding admin buttons or checking a client-side role as security.

The administrator's authorization must be enforced on the server and through appropriate database policies.

6. Supabase Database Design

Design a normalized PostgreSQL schema with appropriate foreign keys, unique constraints, indexes, and timestamps.

6.1 articles

Suggested fields:

- id: UUID primary key.
- title: text.
- slug: text.
- excerpt: text, nullable.
- content: validated article content.
- featured_image_url: text, nullable.
- featured_image_alt: text, nullable.
- seo_title: text, nullable.
- meta_description: text, nullable.
- canonical_url: text, nullable.
- author_name: text, nullable.
- status: draft, published, or scheduled.
- published_at: timestamp, nullable.
- created_at: timestamp.
- updated_at: timestamp.
- noindex: boolean, default false.

Use appropriate uniqueness rules for public article slugs.

6.2 categories

Suggested fields:

- id: UUID primary key.
- name: text.
- slug: text, unique.
- description: text, nullable.
- created_at: timestamp.
- updated_at: timestamp.

6.3 subcategories

Suggested fields:

- id: UUID primary key.
- category_id: UUID foreign key.
- name: text.
- slug: text.
- description: text, nullable.
- created_at: timestamp.
- updated_at: timestamp.

Enforce suitable uniqueness constraints, including parent-category relationships.

6.4 article_categories

Use a join table if articles need to belong to multiple categories.

Otherwise, use a simpler category relationship if the product requires one primary category per article.

Choose one consistent model and implement it throughout the application.

6.5 site_settings

Store manageable website settings, such as:

- Site name.
- Site description.
- Logo URL.
- Favicon URL.
- Default SEO metadata.
- Contact email.
- Default author information.
- Advertising configuration.

Do not store service-role keys, private API credentials, or secrets in this table.

6.6 static_pages

Suggested fields:

- id: UUID primary key.
- title: text.
- slug: text, unique.
- content: text or validated rich content.
- seo_title: text, nullable.
- meta_description: text, nullable.
- updated_at: timestamp.

Protect editable page content through the admin authorization system.

7. Database Security

Security is mandatory.

Implement Row Level Security (RLS) on tables accessible through the Supabase client.

Requirements:

- Public users may read only published articles.
- Public users may read public categories and subcategories.
- Public users must not read drafts or unpublished content.
- Only the authorized administrator may create or modify content.
- Only the authorized administrator may manage categories and settings.
- Private settings and administrative data must remain protected.
- Storage upload and deletion policies must be appropriately restricted.
- Every sensitive server action must verify authorization.

Never expose the Supabase service-role key to the browser.

Use environment variables for credentials.

Client-side visibility checks are not a substitute for authorization.

Do not create an admin role based only on a user-editable metadata field.

Use a trusted authorization mechanism, such as a protected administrator allowlist or a securely managed database role.

Provide database migrations and setup instructions.

8. Rendering, Caching, and Publishing

Use Next.js App Router with server-rendered public article pages where appropriate.

The application must support publishing content dynamically without requiring a complete redeployment for every article.

Implement a clear content invalidation strategy.

When an article is published, edited, unpublished, or deleted:

1. Validate the administrator's authorization.
2. Validate and save the content in Supabase.
3. Update the relevant article page.
4. Invalidate or refresh cached article data.
5. Refresh affected category and subcategory listings.
6. Refresh homepage article listings.
7. Update sitemap data when appropriate.

Use Next.js revalidation APIs where compatible with the selected deployment architecture.

If scheduled publishing is supported, use a reliable scheduled mechanism that can publish due articles even when nobody visits the website.

Do not rely solely on browser timers.

Ensure that unpublished or scheduled content does not accidentally appear publicly.

Document any required Vercel configuration or scheduled jobs.

